    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "Read this next to `F:schedule-critical-chain-infeasible`, whose footprint lists 30 named axioms and which records `axiom_footprint: []` as 'not on offer and cannot be' for this route. It is on offer now, and the difference is not a stronger kernel: it is the same proof term with its assumptions moved from the environment into the theorem. The 30 declarations are still in `arith_prelude.rs` and `nat_axiom_inventory` still reports `real: axiom=30` -- reducing the trusted base was never the goal. What changed is that no reconstructed refutation DEPENDS on it. Measured across five fixtures (three Farkas shapes, one strict cycle, one sum-of-squares), 21 of the 30 are reached by at least one proof; the nine never reached are `le_trans`, `mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left`, `add_lt_add_of_le_of_lt`, `mul_comm`, `mul_assoc`, `mul_one`, `mul_zero`, `left_distrib` and `mul_nonneg`. Scope: the generalization operates on a finished proof term, so it reaches exactly as far as the reconstructors do -- the QF_LIA routes, which have no Farkas path to a kernel at all, are untouched, and `prove_unsat_to_lean_module` still routes a pure-Real conjunctive unsat to the contentless `LraDpll` shim.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
